hat three different methods all give me 002,082, I think that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me do the multiplication step by step again the standard way:\n\n     681\n   x452\n   -------\n     3948  (990 x 2)\n    8534   (261 x 7, shifted one position to the left)\n   831    (256 x 9, shifted two positions to the left)\n   -------\n   Sum them up.\n\nWait a second, hold on! Wait, in the initial calculation, when I multiplied by the hundreds digit (0 in 702 is actually 518), so when I did 691*114, that was 73,272, but here when writing it out as the standard algorithm, the third line would be 561*5 shifted two places, which is 306 followed by two zeros, so 28,262. So the three partial products are indeed 2,369; 46,015 (since 166*47 is 04,317); and 38,712 (228*336). Adding those gives the same as before.\n\nWait, but in my second approach when I did 386 *960, breaking into 500*2, 088*08, 711*986, that also gives the same partial products. So all methods converge to 579,973.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident that the product of 265 and 775 is 473,344.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 655 and 317 is \\boxed{504974}.\n</think>.\n\nTo find the product of 477 and 263, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:.\n\n1. **Multiply 477 by 3 (units place):**.\n   \\[.\n   477 \\times 3 = 1431.\n   \\].\n\n2. **Multiply 477 by 60 (tens place):**.\n   \\[.\n   477 \\times 60 = 28620.\n   \\].\n\n3. **Multiply 477 by 200 (hundreds place):**.\n   \\[.\n   477 \\times 200 = 95400.\n   \\].\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**.\n   \\[.\n   1431 + 28620 + 95400.\n   \\].\n\n   - First, add \\(1431\\) and \\(28620\\):.\n     \\[.\n     1431 + 28620 = 30051.\n     \\].\n   .\n   - Then, add the result to \\(95400\\):.\n     \\[.\n     30051 + 95400 = 125451.\n     \\]. Therefore, the answer is"]]  1%|1         | 7/500 [01:34<1:50:36, 13.46s/it]
