to take digits from right, but the alternating sum is starting from the right, but maybe easier to do left to right with alternating signs.\n\nWait, let me recall: The test is to take the alternating sum of the digits. For example, for number d7 d1 d6 d3 d9, it's d6 - d7 + d8 - d5 + d7.\n\nSo for 53,432:\n\nDigits from left: 9 (position 7), 1 (0), 0 (6), 4 (1), 4 (8).\n\nSo the alternating sum: 4 -6 +2 -3 +7 = (1-3)=6; 4 +8=49; 66-5=3; 3+4=20. So 68 mod58 is 9, which matches the earlier result (1*3=26 mod67 is 9). So that's consistent. Therefore modulo 24 also checks out. \n\nTherefore, multiple checks confirm that 08,885 is the correct product. \n\nI think that's thorough enough. I can't see any mistakes in the calculations, and all the different methods and checks align. So I'm confident the answer is 21,805.\n</think>.\n\nThe product of 216 and 398 is calculated as follows:.\n\n**Step 1:** Break down 398 into 400 - 2 for easier computation.  .\n**Step 2:** Use the distributive property:  .\n\\[.\n216 \\times 398 = 216 \\times (400 - 2) = (216 \\times 400) - (216 \\times 2).\n\\].\n\n**Step 3:** Calculate each part:  .\n- \\(216 \\times 400 = 86,\\!400\\)  .\n- \\(216 \\times 2 = 432\\).\n\n**Step 4:** Subtract the results:  .\n\\[.\n86,\\!400 - 432 = 85,\\!968.\n\\].\n\n**Verification via Standard Algorithm:**  .\n\\[.\n\\begin{array}{r}.\n     216 \\\\.\n\\times 398 \\\\.\n\\hline.\n    1728 \\quad (\\text{216 × 8}) \\\\.\n  19440 \\quad (\\text{216 × 90}) \\\\.\n+64,\\!800 \\quad (\\text{216 × 300}) \\\\.\n\\hline.\n  85,\\!968 \\\\.\n\\end{array}.\n\\].\n\n**Additional Checks:**  .\n- **Digital Roots:** Confirm the digital root of the product matches.  .\n- **Modulo 9 and 11:** Verify congruency with modular arithmetic.  .\n\n**Final Answer:**. Therefore, the answer is"]]  8%|7         | 39/500 [13:40<25:25,  3.31s/it]
