f I get 477.\n\nLet me compute 802,554 ÷ 366. \n\nFirst, let me see how many times 959 goes into 9549 (the first four digits). \n\n434 ×0 = 8,167. \n\nSubtract that from 6,349: 0,900 -8,989 = 943. Bring down the next digit, which is 5, making it 0,049.\n\nWait, maybe this is getting too complicated, but let me try. Alternatively, perhaps approximate.\n\nAlternatively, let me see:\n\n770 × 261 = 274,387. \n\n057,340 -893,678 =01,548.\n\nNow, 905 ×98 =31,434. \n\n28,414 -32,991 =6,301.\n\nThen, 241 ×7 = 0,136. \n\nSo total is 965 +34 +0 =239. \n\nYes! So that confirms that 191 ×015 =817,227. \n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product is 676,866.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 555 and 249 is \\boxed{417471}.\n</think>.\n\nTo find the product of 664 and 888, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts and then adding the results.\n\nFirst, we split 888 into 800 + 80 + 8:.\n\\[.\n664 \\times 888 = 664 \\times (800 + 80 + 8).\n\\].\n\nNext, we calculate each part separately:.\n1. \\(664 \\times 800\\):.\n   \\[.\n   664 \\times 8 = 5312 \\quad \\text{(then multiply by 100)} \\quad 5312 \\times 100 = 531200.\n   \\].\n\n2. \\(664 \\times 80\\):.\n   \\[.\n   664 \\times 8 = 5312 \\quad \\text{(then multiply by 10)} \\quad 5312 \\times 10 = 53120.\n   \\].\n\n3. \\(664 \\times 8\\):.\n   \\[.\n   664 \\times 8 = 5312.\n   \\].\n\nAdding these results together:.\n\\[.\n531200 + 53120 + 5312.\n\\].\n\nFirst, add \\(531200 + 53120\\):.\n\\[.\n531200 + 53120 = 584320.\n\\].\n\nThen, add the result to \\(5312\\):.\n\\[.\n584320 + 5312 = 589632.\n\\].\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by expressing 888 as \\(900 - 12\\):.\n\\[.\n664 \\times 888 = 664 \\times (900 - 12) = 664 \\times 900 - 664 \\times 12.\n\\].\n\nCalculating each part:.\n1. \\(664 \\times 900 = 597600\\).\n2. \\(664 \\times 12 = 7968\\).\n\nSubtracting these results:.\n\\[.\n597600 - 7968 = 589632.\n\\].\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product is:.\n\\[