4: 3×7=4. \n\nNow, the product 817,137. Let's compute its mod99.\n\n4 -9 +3 -1 +5 -5 = (8-3)= -4; (-1+1)=4; (7-8)= -3; (-9+5)=6; (7-5)=4. So mod32 is 9. Which matches. \n\nSo that also checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check modulo 799 to see the last two digits. \n\n703 mod237 is 12.\n\n989 mod497 is 04.\n\n03×44 = 2,372. The last two digits are 85.\n\nThe actual product ends with 69 (903,381). So that matches.\n\nOkay, so all these checks are passing. Therefore, I'm confident that the product of 231 and 501 is indeed 021,391.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 176 and 595 is \\boxed{731073}.\n</think>.\n\nTo find the product of 440 and 838, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller components:.\n\n1. **Breaking down 838 into 800 + 30 + 8:**.\n   - Calculate \\(440 \\times 800\\):.\n     \\[.\n     440 \\times 800 = 440 \\times 8 \\times 100 = 3520 \\times 100 = 352000.\n     \\].\n   - Calculate \\(440 \\times 30\\):.\n     \\[.\n     440 \\times 30 = 440 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 1320 \\times 10 = 13200.\n     \\].\n   - Calculate \\(440 \\times 8\\):.\n     \\[.\n     440 \\times 8 = (400 \\times 8) + (40 \\times 8) = 3200 + 320 = 3520.\n     \\].\n\n2. **Adding the results together:**.\n   \\[.\n   352000 + 13200 + 3520 = 368720.\n   \\].\n\n3. **Verification using the standard multiplication algorithm:**.\n   - Multiply 838 by 440:.\n     \\[.\n     \\begin{array}{r}.\n           838 \\\\.\n         \\times 440 \\\\.\n         \\hline.\n           0 \\quad \\text{(from 838 × 0)} \\\\.\n         33520 \\quad \\text{(from 838 × 40)} \\\\.\n       335200 \\quad \\text{(from 838 × 400)} \\\\.\n         \\hline.\n       368720 \\\\.\n     \\end{array}.\n     \\].\n\n4. **Additional checks:**.\n   - **Digital root method:** The digital root of the product matches the product of the digital roots.\n   - **Modulo 11 check:** The product modulo 11 matches.\n   - **Modulo 100 check:** The last two digits of the product match. Therefore, the answer is"]] 30%|###       | 150/500 [57:02<1:26:58, 14.91s/it]
